WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.069
The Nun delivers a twist at the end of the
movie that will have surprised many moviegoers;

00:00:05.069 --> 00:00:09.260
however, originally, the final scene was going
to be even more shocking.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.530
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video I'll be revealing the incredible

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.810
ending for The Nun that we never got to see,
plus I'll be explaining some other fascinating

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.820
changes from the original script.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:24.019
Make sure you stay till the end where I'll
also reveal the crazy decision Sister Irene

00:00:24.019 --> 00:00:25.949
almost made at the end of the movie!

00:00:25.949 --> 00:00:30.029
If you're finding me for the first time, be
sure to subscribe for all my regular and upcoming

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:32.180
deleted scenes and horror videos.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.680
Spoilers ahead for The Nun, so take care if
you haven't seen it yet.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.260
As I talked about in my ending explained video,
the big reveal at the very end of The Nun

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.989
is that Frenchie is actually Maurice, the
French-Canadian farmer whose exorcism Lorraine

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:47.690
and Ed Warren discussed in the first Conjuring.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:52.129
The scene is mostly a replay of the Warrens
witnessing Maurice's attempted exorcism with

00:00:52.129 --> 00:00:56.410
an added quick reveal that Valak possessed
Frenchie during the showdown at the abbey

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:59.910
and that this is how Lorraine saw that horrifying
vision of Valak.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:05.710
But the original ending as laid out in the
January 2017 script for The Nun was even more

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:06.710
dramatic.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:10.670
This alternate ending is set in a small town
in Massachusetts, USA years after the events

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:11.670
of The Nun.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:15.190
The scene begins with the sound of a woman
screaming and pleading for her life.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:19.370
A shotgun blasts her arm off then there's
a kill shot to her head.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:24.070
The scene then reveals the killer is Frenchie
aka Maurice, who's sweating and has the look

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:25.400
of a man possessed.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.950
Maurice sits down and pulls up his T-shirt
revealing upside-down crosses bulging out

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:31.870
of his body and his eyes start to bleed.

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:35.970
There's a pounding on the front door of the
house which Maurice ignores as he tries to

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.110
shoot himself.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:41.810
However, his gun fails and then Ed and Lorraine
Warren burst through the door together with

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:42.810
a priest.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:46.670
They see Maurice covered in his wife's blood
and a horrified Lorraine looks across the

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:47.920
room at a mirror.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.530
But instead of seeing Maurice's reflection,
she sees Valak staring back at her.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.710
As Lorraine freezes in horror, Valak snarls,
and then the mirror shatters as the screen

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:58.960
cuts to black and the credits roll.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.120
Now, I think this would have been a really
intense scene to watch and not only would

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.330
it have given us a cool cameo from Patrick
Wilson and Vera Farmiga, who's the real-life

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:11.730
sister of Taissa Farmiga who plays Sister
Irene, but this alternate ending would also

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:15.880
have made the connection between Valak and
Lorraine even clearer and more horrifying.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.410
I suspect the filmmakers opted to reuse archive
footage from The Conjuring instead, and reshoot

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:25.950
some close-up footage of the possessed Maurice
with actor Jonas Bloquet who plays Frenchie,

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.940
as it would be a lot cheaper than getting
Vera Farmiga and Patrick Wilson.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.130
Plus there would have been extra costs for
the additional set and effects for Valak's

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:34.280
appearance.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.990
The issue with the alternate ending as it's
written in the original script is that it's

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.920
a little inconsistent with events established
in The Conjuring.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.630
In his university lecture, Ed Warren explained
that Maurice tried to kill his wife but only

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:49.380
managed to shoot her in the arm before turning
the gun on himself.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.410
In this alternate ending for The Nun, it seems
to be the other way around in terms of who

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:54.410
gets killed.

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:57.700
I'm guessing that this was a case of a simple
mix-up in the screenwriter's head and I think

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.460
it would have been corrected if they'd chosen
to go ahead with the scene.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.490
Another big difference in the original ending
is that Frenchie was going to be infected

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.470
via the snake-tongued Daniel.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:12.240
Daniel is the boy that died when Father Burke
tried to exorcise him and, in the earlier

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.790
script, Daniel was going to be a winged demon.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:18.640
In that screenplay, both Sister Irene and
Father Burke would have witnessed the winged

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.653
demon-boy vomit a snake into Frenchie's mouth
during the final fight.

00:03:21.653 --> 00:03:25.819
The alternate script made it clear that the
winged demon-boy was one of the several forms

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:27.790
that Valak takes in the movie.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:31.770
And showing Frenchie's possession by Valak
in this way before we see the demon get sent

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:37.290
back to hell would have cleared up some confusion
about whether it's really Valak inside Frenchie.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:41.750
Sister Irene and Father Burke would also have
seen the upside-down crosses immediately appearing

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:45.310
under Frenchie's skin and his eyes turning
demonic.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.910
Frenchie would also have attacked them during
the final fight before disappearing and not

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.599
being seen again in the movie until that scene
where he kills his wife.

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:57.090
Jonas Bloquet has said that Frenchie's role
in the film was changed after test audiences

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:02.160
reacted well to his character and the filmmakers
decided to give him a more heroic part.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.300
By the way, that original look for the demon-boy
Daniel was a deliberate nod to descriptions

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:11.069
of Valak's appearance in the 17th-century
book of magic the Lesser Key of Solomon.

00:04:11.069 --> 00:04:15.730
In the early 2017 script, the key that Father
Burke discovers on Sister Victoria's dead

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.759
body was a holy relic that gave protection
against Valak's influence.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:24.330
The key literally shone a protective light
that Irene, Burke and Frenchie had to remain

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:28.719
within in order to stay protected as they
descended through the abbey to find the gateway

00:04:28.719 --> 00:04:29.740
to hell.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.520
The original script also tells us how in the
past the Abbess had tried to close the portal

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:39.749
without the key's protection and ended up
dead and under the demonic influence of Valak.

00:04:39.749 --> 00:04:43.910
An interesting addition to the final movie
which wasn't in the original script is the

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.979
use of the blood of Christ to send Valak back
to hell.

00:04:46.979 --> 00:04:51.129
In the original story, the demon's defeat
didn't feature any holy blood.

00:04:51.129 --> 00:04:55.949
Instead, it involved Father Burke using Valak's
name to gain dominion over him and send him

00:04:55.949 --> 00:05:00.210
back to hell, in a very similar way to how
Lorraine cast the demon back at the end of

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:01.509
The Conjuring 2.

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:05.400
The thing with that choice is that it may
have felt too repetitive whereas having Irene

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:10.330
spit holy blood all over Valak instead was
a nice tie-in with how the locals in the village

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:14.749
always spat whenever the abbey was mentioned
in order to ward off evil.

00:05:14.749 --> 00:05:18.879
Another addition to the final movie was director
Corin Hardy's decision to cover the gateway

00:05:18.879 --> 00:05:20.979
to hell with a pool of water.

00:05:20.979 --> 00:05:25.539
Hardy wanted to add a layer of water to give
a sense of having to pass through a transformational

00:05:25.539 --> 00:05:27.139
layer between the two worlds.

00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:31.259
It was an interesting change and resulted
in some very atmospheric and creepy scenes

00:05:31.259 --> 00:05:34.009
such as Valak walking on water.

00:05:34.009 --> 00:05:37.680
The twist that the abbey's nuns were dead
all along is only revealed in the visually

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.710
impressive scene in the chapel where Irene
keeps a prayer vigil with a group of nuns

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:43.870
in order to hold back Valak.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:47.849
In the original script, the reveal is not
quite as spectacular although the twist is

00:05:47.849 --> 00:05:49.240
probably clearer.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:54.150
The scene simply involves Irene talking to
several nuns in a room, after which Burke

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:57.759
and Frenchie arrive and Irene discovers nobody
was actually there with her.

00:05:57.759 --> 00:06:01.809
There were also additional scenes where Irene,
Burke and Frenchie discovered the bodies of

00:06:01.809 --> 00:06:06.060
dead tortured nuns as they descended deeper
into the abbey, which would have been more

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.069
obvious evidence than we get in the final
film that all the nuns had been killed before

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:11.900
Irene and Burke arrived.

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.599
By the way, if you're interested in learning
more about exactly how the twist worked and

00:06:15.599 --> 00:06:19.439
how it was foreshadowed, check out my Twist
and Ending Explained video.

00:06:19.439 --> 00:06:23.460
Another major change for Irene's story in
the movie was that she was never possessed

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:28.159
by Valak during the final fight and, at the
end, she decides to stay on by herself at

00:06:28.159 --> 00:06:30.690
the abbey with the intention of rebuilding
it.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:35.129
And she even dons a black habit previously
worn by one of the dead nuns!

00:06:35.129 --> 00:06:38.879
To be honest, this ending for Irene would
have felt very creepy as the thought of her

00:06:38.879 --> 00:06:42.849
staying there with the portal to hell just
underneath the abbey while she wears a dead

00:06:42.849 --> 00:06:44.869
nun's habit gives me the chills!

00:06:44.869 --> 00:06:49.360
So, what do you think about the alternate
endings and changes from the original screenplay?

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:53.460
Do you think they would have improved the
final film or do you prefer what you saw on

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:54.460
screen?

